  <Pages>

    <Page url="/">
      <VStack padding="2rem" gap="1.5rem">
        <Markdown>
          <![CDATA[

Every XMLUI component that wraps a React component needs plumbing — a **renderer** that bridges XMLUI markup to React props, plus **state management** that handles value lifecycle (`initialValue` parsing, `updateState` calls, external value syncing, component API registration). Today, all of this is written by hand.

For a component like **Slider**, wrapping Radix's slider for XMLUI requires:

- A **renderer** (~40 lines): calling `extractValue` for each prop, `lookupEventHandler` for each event, `lookupSyncCallback` for each callback, and manually forwarding plumbing like `className`, `registerComponentApi`, `updateState`, and `state.value`
- A **native component** (~347 lines): `useState`/`useEffect` for `initialValue` parsing, value syncing, `updateState` calls, `registerComponentApi` wiring, and the actual Radix assembly

This approach has three problems:

1. **Boilerplate.** The mapping code is tedious to write and review.

2. **Incomplete coverage.** Because every prop must be explicitly listed in the renderer, props that the native React component already supports get silently dropped.

3. **Duplicated plumbing.** The state lifecycle code (parse initial value, sync external changes, call updateState, register APIs) is nearly identical across every stateful component, but each one reimplements it from scratch.

## A first-pass solution

`wrapComponent` is a utility that generates the renderer automatically. You declare only the exceptions — which props are booleans, which are events, which need renaming — and everything else passes through by default.

## This demo

- **Slider** is the hand-written original (40-line renderer).
- **SliderW** uses `wrapComponent` (13 lines), wrapping the exact same `SliderNative.tsx`.

The <a href="/slider-and-sliderw" target="_blank">Slider and SliderW</a> page confirms they behave identically for all currently-wrapped features.

The <a href="/slider-vs-sliderw" target="_blank">Slider vs SliderW</a> page shows what **SliderW** can do that **Slider** can't. Here is every prop in the `SliderNative` Props type that the hand-written renderer silently drops:

| Dropped prop | What it does |
|---|---|
| **inverted** | Reverses the slider direction (Radix UI) |
| **style** | Direct CSS styles on the slider container |
| **tabIndex** | Controls keyboard focus order |

`SliderNative` also spreads `...rest` onto its outer `<div>`, so standard HTML attributes are accepted but never reach it:

| Dropped HTML attr | What it does |
|---|---|
| **title** | Native browser tooltip on hover |
| **aria-label** | Accessible name for screen readers and test automation |
| **aria-describedby** | Links to a description element |

With `wrapComponent`, all of these forward automatically because unrecognized props flow through `extractValue` by default.

## A second-pass solution: `wrapCompound`

`wrapComponent` eliminates the hand-written renderer, but you still write **SliderNative.tsx** (347 lines) — the React component that manages local value state, parses `initialValue`, syncs external value changes, calls `updateState`, registers component APIs, and assembles the Radix compound structure.

Look at what's in those 347 lines:

- `useState` + `useEffect` to parse and set `initialValue` on mount
- `useEffect` to sync when external `state.value` changes
- `updateState({ value })` calls to push changes back to XMLUI
- `registerComponentApi` wiring for `focus()` and `setValue()`
- Clamping and formatting logic for min/max bounds

None of that is slider-specific. Every Radix compound component (RadioGroup, Toggle, Accordion, Tabs) needs the same state lifecycle. The only part that's genuinely unique to Slider is the Radix assembly: `Root > Track > Range > Thumb[]` with tooltip wiring.

`wrapCompound` is a new function that creates a **StateWrapper** — a React component that handles all of the above generically. It receives two optional hooks in its config:

- **`parseInitialValue(raw, props)`** — component-specific parsing (e.g., Slider needs to parse JSON arrays and clamp to min/max)
- **`formatExternalValue(value, props)`** — normalize incoming values (e.g., ensure they're arrays, clamped to bounds)

The StateWrapper manages local state, fires `updateState` on changes, and passes three clean props to your render component: `value`, `onChange`, and `registerApi`. Your component never imports anything from XMLUI — it's pure React.

## What you write at each stage

Today, wrapping Radix's slider for XMLUI requires two files: **SliderNative.tsx** (the React component — 347 lines of state management, value parsing, updateState plumbing, and Radix assembly) plus **Slider.tsx** (the hand-written renderer — 40 lines of prop mapping). Total: **387 lines**.

`wrapComponent` eliminates the renderer. You still write SliderNative.tsx unchanged, but replace the 40-line renderer with a 13-line config. Total: **360 lines**.

`wrapCompound` eliminates the XMLUI plumbing from the native component. You write only **SliderRender.tsx** — 109 lines of pure React that receives `value`, `onChange`, and `registerApi` and just assembles the Radix compound structure. No `updateState`, no `initialValue` useEffect, no value sync, no XMLUI imports. Total: **109 lines**.

The test we built in phase 1 validated the final refactor. Same baselines, same generated Playwright assertions, same semantic trace comparison. `slider-pass` still PASSES, `slider-fail` still FAILS with the correct value mismatch. The behavioral contract is preserved across the rewrite.
]]>
        </Markdown>

        <ExpandableItem
          summary="What you write today: Slider.tsx + SliderNative.tsx (387 lines)">
          <Markdown>
            <![CDATA[
**Slider.tsx** — the hand-written renderer (40 lines):
```typescript
export const sliderComponentRenderer = createComponentRenderer(
  COMP,
  SliderMd,
  ({
    node, extractValue, lookupEventHandler, lookupSyncCallback,
    className, updateState, state, registerComponentApi,
  }) => {
    return (
      <Slider
        validationStatus={extractValue(node.props.validationStatus)}
        minStepsBetweenThumbs={extractValue(node.props?.minStepsBetweenThumbs)}
        value={state.value}
        initialValue={extractValue(node.props.initialValue)}
        updateState={updateState}
        onDidChange={lookupEventHandler("didChange")}
        onFocus={lookupEventHandler("gotFocus")}
        onBlur={lookupEventHandler("lostFocus")}
        registerComponentApi={registerComponentApi}
        className={className}
        step={extractValue(node.props?.step)}
        min={extractValue(node.props?.minValue)}
        max={extractValue(node.props?.maxValue)}
        enabled={extractValue.asOptionalBoolean(node.props?.enabled)}
        autoFocus={extractValue.asOptionalBoolean(node.props.autoFocus)}
        readOnly={extractValue.asOptionalBoolean(node.props.readOnly)}
        required={extractValue.asOptionalBoolean(node.props.required)}
        rangeStyle={extractValue(node.props?.rangeStyle)}
        thumbStyle={extractValue(node.props?.thumbStyle)}
        showValues={extractValue.asOptionalBoolean(node.props?.showValues)}
        valueFormat={lookupSyncCallback(node.props?.valueFormat)}
      />
    );
  },
);
```

Plus **SliderNative.tsx** — 347 lines including state management, initialValue parsing, value syncing, updateState calls, registerComponentApi, tooltip state, and Radix assembly. Every prop must be explicitly listed in both files.
]]>
          </Markdown>
        </ExpandableItem>

        <ExpandableItem
          summary="What you write with wrapComponent: SliderNative.tsx + config (360 lines)">
          <Markdown>
            <![CDATA[
The 40-line renderer is replaced by a 13-line config:
```typescript
export const sliderWrappedComponentRenderer = wrapComponent("SliderW", Slider, SliderMd, {
  booleans: ["enabled", "autoFocus", "readOnly", "required", "showValues"],
  events: {
    didChange: "onDidChange",
    gotFocus: "onFocus",
    lostFocus: "onBlur",
  },
  callbacks: {
    valueFormat: "valueFormat",
  },
  rename: {
    minValue: "min",
    maxValue: "max",
  },
});
```

But you still write the full **SliderNative.tsx** (347 lines) unchanged — all the XMLUI plumbing stays.
]]>
          </Markdown>
        </ExpandableItem>

        <ExpandableItem
          summary="What you write with wrapCompound: SliderRender.tsx (109 lines)">
          <Markdown>
            <![CDATA[
Pure React. No XMLUI imports. Receives `value`, `onChange`, and `registerApi` from the engine.
```typescript
import React, { useCallback, useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { forwardRef } from "react";
import { Root, Range, Track, Thumb } from "@radix-ui/react-slider";
import classnames from "classnames";
import styles from "./Slider.module.scss";
import { Tooltip } from "../Tooltip/TooltipNative";

export const SliderRender = forwardRef(({
  value, onChange, registerApi, className,
  min = 0, max = 10, step = 1, inverted, enabled = true, readOnly,
  required, autoFocus, tabIndex = -1, validationStatus = "none",
  minStepsBetweenThumbs = 1, rangeStyle, thumbStyle,
  showValues = true, valueFormat = (v: number) => v.toString(),
  id, ...rest
}: any, ref: any) => {
  const inputRef = useRef<any>(null);
  const thumbsRef = useRef<(HTMLSpanElement | null)[]>([]);
  const [showTooltip, setShowTooltip] = useState(false);

  const displayValue: number[] = Array.isArray(value)
    ? value : value != null ? [value] : [min];

  useEffect(() => {
    registerApi?.({
      focus: () => {
        const thumb = thumbsRef.current.find(t => t !== null);
        if (thumb) thumb.focus();
        else inputRef.current?.focus();
      },
      setValue: (v: any) => onChange?.(v),
    });
  }, [registerApi, onChange]);

  const handleValueChange = useCallback((vals: number[]) => {
    if (readOnly) return;
    if (inputRef.current) inputRef.current.value = vals;
    onChange(vals.length === 1 ? vals[0] : vals);
  }, [onChange, readOnly]);

  return (
    <div {...rest} ref={ref}
      className={classnames(styles.sliderContainer, className)}>
      <Root ref={inputRef} value={displayValue}
        onValueChange={handleValueChange}
        min={min} max={max} step={step} inverted={inverted}
        disabled={!enabled} tabIndex={tabIndex}
        aria-readonly={readOnly}
        minStepsBetweenThumbs={minStepsBetweenThumbs}
        className={classnames(styles.sliderRoot, {
          [styles.disabled]: !enabled, [styles.readOnly]: readOnly,
        })}
        onFocus={(e) => { setShowTooltip(true); rest.onFocus?.(e); }}
        onBlur={(e) => { rest.onBlur?.(e); }}
        onMouseOver={() => setShowTooltip(true)}
        onMouseLeave={() => setShowTooltip(false)}
        onPointerDown={() => setShowTooltip(true)}>
        <Track data-track
          className={classnames(styles.sliderTrack, {
            [styles.disabled]: !enabled, [styles.readOnly]: readOnly,
            [styles.error]: validationStatus === "error",
            [styles.warning]: validationStatus === "warning",
            [styles.valid]: validationStatus === "valid",
          })} style={rangeStyle ? { ...rangeStyle } : undefined}>
          <Range data-range className={classnames(styles.sliderRange, {
            [styles.disabled]: !enabled,
          })} />
        </Track>
        {displayValue.map((_, index) => (
          <Tooltip key={index} text={valueFormat(displayValue[index])}
            delayDuration={100} open={showValues && showTooltip}>
            <Thumb id={id} aria-required={required}
              ref={(el) => { thumbsRef.current[index] = el; }}
              className={classnames(styles.sliderThumb, {
                [styles.disabled]: !enabled,
              })}
              style={thumbStyle ? { ...thumbStyle } : undefined}
              data-thumb-index={index}
              autoFocus={autoFocus && index === 0} />
          </Tooltip>
        ))}
      </Root>
    </div>
  );
});
```
]]>
          </Markdown>
        </ExpandableItem>

        <Markdown>
          <![CDATA[
## Beyond Radix

Everything above wraps Radix UI components. But `wrapCompound` is library-agnostic — it handles the XMLUI state lifecycle for *any* React component. To prove the point:

- <a href="/knob" target="_blank">**Knob**</a> wraps `react-knob-headless` — a circular dial input. ~80 lines of pure React.
- <a href="/code-editor" target="_blank">**CodeEditor**</a> wraps `@monaco-editor/react` — the VS Code editor engine. ~60 lines of pure React.
- <a href="/tiptap-editor" target="_blank">**TiptapEditor**</a> wraps `@tiptap/react` — the ProseMirror-based rich-text framework. ~280 lines of pure React for a full markdown editor with toolbar, tables, task lists, and undo/redo. The existing [Build a TableEditor](https://docs.xmlui.org/build-editor-component) how-to took 14 steps and ~250 lines for table editing alone.

Same pattern, four completely different libraries (Radix, react-knob-headless, Monaco, Tiptap). Each render component receives `value`, `onChange`, `registerApi` — no XMLUI imports needed.
]]>
        </Markdown>

